00:13:57,088 --> 00:13:58,650
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he ends up wondering these things.

159
00:13:58,771 --> 00:14:00,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Was I in the past?

160
00:14:00,974 --> 00:14:01,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Or was I not?

161
00:14:02,056 --> 00:14:03,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I be in the future?

162
00:14:04,399 --> 00:14:04,720
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

163
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:06,162
[SPEAKER_01]: In what way?

164
00:14:06,582 --> 00:14:12,752
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that revolves around the fact that he completely taken for granted that he is.

165
00:14:14,857 --> 00:14:17,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, now he's just wondering, how is he?

166
00:14:18,279 --> 00:14:19,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Or was he all the time?

167
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:20,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But he never questions that.

168
00:14:21,162 --> 00:14:21,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

169
00:14:21,982 --> 00:14:36,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that question of what am I, who am I, when I was, how I was, that already revolves around the present notion of self, present notion of control, which means he's not properly attending to the nature of experience and he doesn't see it as impermanent.

170
00:14:36,813 --> 00:14:39,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing it as impermanent means seeing it as not in your control.

171
00:14:42,485 --> 00:14:44,186
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you have doubts arising.

172
00:14:44,306 --> 00:14:45,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, he has all those doubts.

173
00:14:45,666 --> 00:14:46,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, of course.

174
00:14:46,186 --> 00:14:48,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

175
00:14:48,507 --> 00:14:52,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, he makes himself liable to that doubt.

176
00:14:54,549 --> 00:15:07,292
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, he doesn't recognize that any of those, all of those questions, no matter what the particular thing that they might be asking is, all those questions, in their nature, revolve around the notion of sense of self.

177
00:15:07,873 --> 00:15:08,973
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a mistake.

178
00:15:10,491 --> 00:15:13,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, he'll then just doubt, because none of the answers can be satisfying.

179
00:15:15,273 --> 00:15:15,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Was I?

180
00:15:15,613 --> 00:15:16,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I was.

181
00:15:16,193 --> 00:15:17,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but was I always?

182
00:15:17,494 --> 00:15:18,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know.

183
00:15:18,995 --> 00:15:19,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I be?

184
00:15:19,615 --> 00:15:20,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know that either.

185
00:15:20,935 --> 00:15:21,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I now?

186
00:15:21,636 --> 00:15:22,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I not now?

187
00:15:22,696 --> 00:15:24,457
[SPEAKER_01]: How am I now?

188
00:15:24,517 --> 00:15:29,159
[SPEAKER_01]: All this, oh, it's just just just permutations of the same same assumption.

189
00:15:29,579 --> 00:15:32,840
[SPEAKER_00]: But he goes beyond doubt to be attenched rightly.

190
00:15:33,201 --> 00:15:35,462
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes beyond doubt if he basically

191
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:46,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if he's basically not concerned with those questions, because they're rooted in mistaken assumptions.

192
00:15:47,444 --> 00:15:53,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if he attends rightly, he addresses the mistaken assumptions of sense of control, permanence, and I am.

193
00:15:55,547 --> 00:16:01,770
[SPEAKER_01]: By dissolving that, everything that was depending upon it, namely those questions, cannot remain.

194
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:07,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't answer the questions, so the sotāpanna, basically, makes them cease.

195
00:16:09,512 --> 00:16:11,412
[SPEAKER_01]: His understanding makes all the questions cease.

196
00:16:11,893 --> 00:16:13,013
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes beyond the doubt.

197
00:16:17,294 --> 00:16:33,117
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, after that, a well-taught noble disciple, who has regard for noble ones and is skilled and disciplined in their dhamma, who has regard for true men and is skilled and disciplined in their dhamma, understands what things are fit for attention and what things are unfit for attention.

198
00:16:34,285 --> 00:16:37,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Since that is so, he does not attend to those things unfit for attention.

199
00:16:38,548 --> 00:16:43,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And he attends to those things fit for attention.

200
00:16:44,032 --> 00:16:45,673
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the way he attends.

201
00:16:47,174 --> 00:16:47,975
[SPEAKER_00]: This is suffering.

202
00:16:48,775 --> 00:16:50,477
[SPEAKER_00]: He attends properly.

203
00:16:50,897 --> 00:16:52,558
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the origin of suffering.

204
00:16:52,718 --> 00:16:53,579
[SPEAKER_00]: He attends properly.

205
00:16:53,659 --> 00:16:55,200
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the cessation of suffering.

206
00:16:56,604 --> 00:16:58,905
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.

207
00:16:59,566 --> 00:17:04,308
[SPEAKER_00]: When he attends properly in this way, three fetters are abandoning him.

208
00:17:04,888 --> 00:17:09,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Personality view, doubt, and adherence to rules and observances.

209
00:17:11,212 --> 00:17:13,193
[SPEAKER_00]: These are called the tenets of śrīmad-bhāgavatam.

210
00:17:14,814 --> 00:17:25,980
[SPEAKER_00]: This is interesting because he's saying he's a noble disciple who attends in this way, properly, the Four Noble Truths, but then, when he does this,

211
00:17:26,823 --> 00:17:29,865
[SPEAKER_00]: He reaches the fruit of sotāpanna.

212
00:17:30,706 --> 00:17:30,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

213
00:17:31,006 --> 00:17:35,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because there was the... yeah, there's... in that other sutta, there is a distinction.

214
00:17:35,209 --> 00:17:38,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Before a person becomes sotāpanna, he gets attained to the right view.

215
00:17:39,772 --> 00:17:41,413
[SPEAKER_01]: So, dhammanu sāri, sādhanu sāri.

216
00:17:42,654 --> 00:17:47,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's not sotāpanna yet, but his view is basically now along the right lines.

217
00:17:48,208 --> 00:17:49,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means he knows what the proper attention is.

218
00:17:50,169 --> 00:18:00,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as that very passage says, when he practices it, he comes to understand this suffering, this is the origin, this is the cessation, this is the way that leads to God.

219
00:18:01,414 --> 00:18:04,576
[SPEAKER_01]: But that... Like, those are four things.

220
00:18:04,776 --> 00:18:05,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, four normal truths.

221
00:18:06,317 --> 00:18:09,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're basically all revolving around the same insight.

222
00:18:10,599 --> 00:18:12,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Insight into freedom from suffering.

223
00:18:14,501 --> 00:18:17,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understanding all the aspects of that,

224
00:18:19,072 --> 00:18:21,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Can be put into four Noble Truths.

225
00:18:21,074 --> 00:18:22,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand what it is.

226
00:18:23,336 --> 00:18:25,458
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand what it makes it be.

227
00:18:26,218 --> 00:18:28,000
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand what it makes it not be.

228
00:18:29,121 --> 00:18:31,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you understand the way that leads out of it.

229
00:18:35,907 --> 00:18:40,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you can connect that to what we just said before.

230
00:18:44,594 --> 00:18:45,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Appropriating.

231
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:53,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own life, your own body, your own experience, regarding it as yours, that makes you liable to suffering.

232
00:18:54,464 --> 00:19:07,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily suffer on the immediate level, like even if there is nothing unpleasant bothering you, the fact that you have appropriated these things means there is always a looming possibility of suffering.

233
00:19:07,491 --> 00:19:08,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself is suffering.

234
00:19:09,332 --> 00:19:10,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what he recognizes.

235
00:19:10,933 --> 00:19:12,154
[SPEAKER_01]: This is suffering.

236
00:19:17,105 --> 00:19:22,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense of self, I am, cannot stand on its own.

237
00:19:23,190 --> 00:19:30,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It always needs, and actually, it's founded upon a foundation, a relationship.

238
00:19:31,017 --> 00:19:32,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It's founded upon a relationship with the world.

239
00:19:34,020 --> 00:19:37,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And that relationship is, for me, mine.

240
00:19:40,235 --> 00:19:48,620
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you recognize then, that your sense of self depends upon the world, and when I say the world, I mean experience as a whole.

241
00:19:48,640 --> 00:19:55,284
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, your sights, your sounds, smell, taste, touches, things that you're experiencing, that's what your sense of self depends on.

242
00:19:55,985 --> 00:20:05,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you then start reflecting and seeing, attempting to properly see, these things that you see, hear, taste, touch, are fundamentally out of your control.

243
00:20:06,985 --> 00:20:13,589
[SPEAKER_01]: You might exercise degree of the volition once a thing has arisen.

244
00:20:14,350 --> 00:20:22,035
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that volition is within much greater image of non-control.

245
00:20:22,955 --> 00:20:23,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

246
00:20:23,256 --> 00:20:27,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, your heart can stop any second.

247
00:20:27,058 --> 00:20:30,821
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't even conceive having a say in that.

248
00:20:32,842 --> 00:20:34,043
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, your whole life

249
00:20:35,370 --> 00:20:45,073
[SPEAKER_01]: All the things you experienced, all the things that you will experience, all the things you're experiencing right now, depends upon that beating muscle in your chest that pumps blood.

250
00:20:45,714 --> 00:20:46,754
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

251
00:20:46,814 --> 00:20:53,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It's as random as this stream around us that kind of runs over these rocks and moves the leaves and branches or whatever.

252
00:20:54,617 --> 00:20:55,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It's this movement.

253
00:20:56,437 --> 00:20:58,078
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can also see as a memory.

254
00:20:58,678 --> 00:21:03,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't remember that putting your heart together, putting your body together.

255
00:21:04,943 --> 00:21:09,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, becoming that body, or... you know, as you just had it.

256
00:21:09,606 --> 00:21:11,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it was just given to you.

257
00:21:11,627 --> 00:21:12,688
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just given to you.

258
00:21:13,308 --> 00:21:19,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no say in its assembly, you have no say in its operating, and you certainly have no say in its ending.

259
00:21:20,192 --> 00:21:21,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you regard it as yours.

260
00:21:22,613 --> 00:21:26,035
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that is the discrepancy at the most fundamental existential level.

261
00:21:27,276 --> 00:21:30,118
[SPEAKER_01]: You undo that discrepancy, means you're attending things properly.

262
00:21:31,427 --> 00:21:35,410
[SPEAKER_01]: That means your attention does not maintain that mistake.

263
00:21:35,950 --> 00:21:40,794
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not maintain that discrepancy, which is a wrong attention.

264
00:21:41,975 --> 00:21:43,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's attention of everybody.

265
00:21:43,316 --> 00:21:47,939
[SPEAKER_01]: That patujana attends and every attention maintains that wrong order.

266
00:21:50,341 --> 00:21:52,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Me first, my experience second.

267
00:21:53,623 --> 00:21:55,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance being censored.

268
00:21:55,625 --> 00:21:56,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yes.

269
00:21:56,505 --> 00:21:58,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the result of that...

270
00:21:59,698 --> 00:22:20,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeing that you can't even own these things, even if you want to, no matter how hard you want to, you recognize, oh, thinking that I own them, or rather, entertaining the notions of ownership, well, that's what suffering is.

271
00:22:23,053 --> 00:22:25,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you recognize, this is suffering,

272
00:22:26,628 --> 00:22:32,813
[SPEAKER_01]: The arising of suffering is this very notion of ownership in regard to things that cannot be owned.

273
00:22:34,314 --> 00:22:40,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, consequently, the cessation of suffering is not the cessation of things that cannot be owned.

274
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:46,884
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the Buddha did not have to kill himself in order to understand the nature of life.

275
00:22:47,044 --> 00:22:47,224
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

276
00:22:47,384 --> 00:22:50,106
[SPEAKER_01]: While you are alive, you understand the cessation of life.

277
00:22:50,867 --> 00:22:53,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of life is cessation of appropriation of life.

278
00:22:54,931 --> 00:23:03,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he understands that cessation of suffering is removing of appropriation in regard to your life, your existence.

279
00:23:03,677 --> 00:23:04,477
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't own it.

280
00:23:05,097 --> 00:23:09,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Removing the ownership, disowning it, not attaining it.

281
00:23:10,021 --> 00:23:12,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, how you fulfill that, that's the four noble truth.

282
00:23:12,983 --> 00:23:17,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the path that leads to this fulfillment, to this cessation of suffering.

283
00:23:18,666 --> 00:23:24,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's basically the the everything that revolves around the right view, and right attention, and right mindfulness.

284
00:23:26,143 --> 00:23:45,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that through right view, right attention and right mindfulness, constant practice, it will eventually saturate your whole life, regardless of the circumstances, regardless of the feelings that have arisen, regardless of the pleasant, unpleasant, agreeable, disagreeable perception, regardless of everything.

285
00:23:46,473 --> 00:23:49,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view, right intention, right mindfulness has been brought to fulfillment.

286
00:23:50,178 --> 00:24:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: There will not be any kind of rekindling, so to speak, of the wrong order, of that mistaken notion of ownership, of that habit, basically, of ownership, of that discrepancy.

287
00:24:07,099 --> 00:24:10,642
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when Noble Eightfold Path has been practiced to its fulfillment.

288
00:24:11,322 --> 00:24:17,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are no traces of that discrepancy left, of those wrong notions left, in any regard.

289
00:24:18,108 --> 00:24:22,911
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to any sense, or any thought, or any memory, or any future plan.

290
00:24:23,472 --> 00:24:27,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all saturated with the right attention, with the right mindfulness, with the right view.

291
00:24:28,796 --> 00:24:31,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, obviously, means right effort, right livelihood, and so on.

292
00:24:32,659 --> 00:24:33,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Right concentration.

293
00:24:37,177 --> 00:24:37,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Right composure.

294
00:24:38,858 --> 00:24:39,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Right composure.

295
00:24:39,639 --> 00:24:46,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a better translation, yeah.

296
00:24:46,863 --> 00:24:51,887
[SPEAKER_00]: He goes on to say about the taints to be abandoned by restraining.

297
00:24:52,507 --> 00:24:56,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Here Bhikkhu reflecting wisely, abides with the eye, faculty restrained.

298
00:24:57,390 --> 00:25:08,832
[SPEAKER_00]: While taint's vexation fever might arise in one who abides with the higher faculty unrestrained, there are no taints of vexational fever in one who abides with the higher faculty restrained.

299
00:25:11,132 --> 00:25:13,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, likewise with all the other senses.

300
00:25:16,613 --> 00:25:17,233
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite simple.

301
00:25:17,893 --> 00:25:18,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, yeah.

302
00:25:18,674 --> 00:25:19,454
[SPEAKER_00]: It's straightforward, yeah.

303
00:25:20,234 --> 00:25:24,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, once the right view is established, that won't work.

304
00:25:24,234 --> 00:25:26,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, none of that will work for somebody who doesn't have the right view.

305
00:25:28,283 --> 00:25:34,228
[SPEAKER_01]: As the sutta opens up, it's the one for the one who sees, one who knows and one who sees.

306
00:25:35,389 --> 00:25:43,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once the right view has established, then the one with the right view recognizes, okay, now it's about guarding the senses, practicing the restraint.

307
00:25:44,115 --> 00:25:56,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, none of the mistaken notions, the habits of mistaken notions and discrepancies that are spread or maintained through lost aversion and delusion, none of those will be arising.

308
00:25:57,529 --> 00:25:58,590
[SPEAKER_01]: You're practicing restraint.

309
00:25:59,830 --> 00:26:04,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You stick to there long enough, and then this... they just... it becomes impossible for them to arise.

310
00:26:05,973 --> 00:26:10,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like... like... basically having a habit, and then doing... kind of getting rid of the habit.

311
00:26:11,936 --> 00:26:21,121
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to stick to the opposite behavior, or to the restraint of the habit, for a certain amount of time, depending of how those have... how strong those habits were.

312
00:26:23,108 --> 00:26:31,531
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you stick to it, and practice restraint and so on, and those habits stop arising, that's it?

313
00:26:33,392 --> 00:26:38,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the Noble Disciple writes proper attention.

314
00:26:41,777 --> 00:26:43,378
[SPEAKER_00]: It tends to be abandoned by using.

315
00:26:43,819 --> 00:27:00,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, Bhikkhu, reflecting properly, uses the robe only for protection from cold, and protection from heat, and contact with flies, mosquitoes, wind, and the sun, and creeping things, and only for the purpose of concealing the private parts.

316
00:27:01,789 --> 00:27:19,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting properly, he uses alms food, neither for amusement, nor for intoxication, nor for the sake of physical beauty and attractiveness, but only for the endurance and continuance of this body, for ending discomfort and for the assisting of the holy life.

317
00:27:20,263 --> 00:27:28,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Considering thus, I shall terminate old feelings without arousing new feelings, and I shall be healthy and blameless and shall live in comfort.

318
00:27:31,047 --> 00:27:35,968
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean by not arousing new feelings?

319
00:27:36,008 --> 00:27:38,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like the feeling of hunger.

320
00:27:40,849 --> 00:27:41,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaking too much?

321
00:27:41,890 --> 00:27:49,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, so you eat until the old feeling disappears, of hunger, but you don't overeat, so then you have a new feeling of discomfort of being overeaten.

322
00:27:53,267 --> 00:28:11,616
[SPEAKER_00]: He uses the resting places, dwellings, only for protection from cold, from heat, for protection from contact with flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, and creeping things, and only for the purpose of warding off the perils of climate and for enjoying retreat.

323
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:23,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting properly, he uses the medicinal requisites only for protection from arisen afflicting feelings and for the benefit of good health.

324
00:28:25,070 --> 00:28:37,299
[SPEAKER_00]: While taints, vexation and fever might arise in one who does not use the requisites, thus, there are no taints, vexation or fever in one who uses them, thus, these are called the taints that should be abandoned by using.

325
00:28:41,250 --> 00:28:45,532
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, most of the time, in monasteries, that's what you try to do.

326
00:28:45,572 --> 00:28:49,113
[SPEAKER_00]: You try to use those requisites as a monk, like that.

327
00:28:50,994 --> 00:28:56,716
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a... before you have proper attention.

328
00:28:56,936 --> 00:28:57,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

329
00:28:58,617 --> 00:29:01,178
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's something to aspire, sure.

330
00:29:01,738 --> 00:29:08,021
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, if you want that to result in the abandoning of the taints,

331
00:29:09,604 --> 00:29:10,625
[SPEAKER_01]: The right view is necessary.

332
00:29:10,825 --> 00:29:12,667
[SPEAKER_01]: The right view comes first.

333
00:29:12,707 --> 00:29:14,949
[SPEAKER_01]: We've said that many times.

334
00:29:14,989 --> 00:29:15,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to come first.

335
00:29:16,230 --> 00:29:18,552
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know what you're supposed to do, how can you do it?

336
00:29:19,153 --> 00:29:19,593
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

337
00:29:19,693 --> 00:29:20,434
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

338
00:29:22,936 --> 00:29:30,783
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know what a dhamma is, how can you practice it?

339
00:29:30,823 --> 00:29:32,945
[SPEAKER_00]: It pertains to be abandoned by enduring.

340
00:29:35,037 --> 00:29:43,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Here a bhikkhu, acting properly, bears cold and heat, hunger and thirst, and contact with flies, mosquitoes, wind and sun, creeping things.

341
00:29:43,401 --> 00:29:54,667
[SPEAKER_00]: He endures ill-spoken, unwelcomed words, and arisen bodily feelings that are painful, sharp, piercing, disagreeable, distressing, and menacing to life.

342
00:29:56,408 --> 00:30:04,632
[SPEAKER_00]: While taints, vexation, and fever might arise in one who does not induce things, there are no taints, vexation, or fever in one who endures them.

343
00:30:08,722 --> 00:30:17,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how does he endure, he endures the weather, uncomfortable, uncomfortable feelings, painful experiences?

344
00:30:17,986 --> 00:30:18,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

345
00:30:19,866 --> 00:30:25,628
[SPEAKER_00]: By becoming, you know, a lot of people can endure these things, can sort of... Again, again... go to the army.

346
00:30:25,648 --> 00:30:32,591
[SPEAKER_01]: He will not, yeah, exactly, but it will not result in destruction of lust, aversion and delusion, if there is no right view.

347
00:30:33,651 --> 00:30:36,152
[SPEAKER_01]: With the right view, and with the hardship,

348
00:30:37,886 --> 00:30:56,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that was dependent, any of those habits, any of those discrepancies, they were kind of hiding about, dependent upon cherishing the notions of comfort, for example, will be destroyed with the right view that puts one through discomfort.

349
00:30:56,561 --> 00:31:00,825
[SPEAKER_01]: So, discomfort founded upon the right view will destroy any of the things.

350
00:31:02,846 --> 00:31:04,828
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean that the things were missing there.

351
00:31:06,327 --> 00:31:12,311
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in case there were, they would be destroyed.

352
00:31:12,331 --> 00:31:29,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, that's why the Buddha sometimes, like, for some monks, with the right view, ascetic practice, uh, dūtangas, uh, would be resulting in, uh, in, uh, in arahantship, in developing further wisdom.

353
00:31:29,561 --> 00:31:32,263
[SPEAKER_01]: For some other monks, with the right view, it wouldn't.

354
00:31:32,903 --> 00:31:35,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So he said, let those who want

355
00:31:36,794 --> 00:31:40,118
[SPEAKER_01]: To exert themselves in such practices, exert themselves in such practices.

356
00:31:40,739 --> 00:31:43,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, either way, the right view needs to be there.

357
00:31:44,364 --> 00:31:51,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because, you know, people's habits have evolved differently, people's lusts have proliferated differently.

358
00:31:51,954 --> 00:31:54,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the existential discrepancy is the same.

359
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:58,862
[SPEAKER_01]: But the particular manifestations of it, yeah, that needs to be addressed.

360
00:31:59,243 --> 00:32:09,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically like, like, you know, you might not necessarily target one by one, but you put yourself out through some discomfort in case things are there.

361
00:32:09,810 --> 00:32:13,892
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically like taking antibiotics to wipe the harmful bacteria.

362
00:32:17,535 --> 00:32:19,836
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't aim at one or two.

363
00:32:20,336 --> 00:32:22,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, just you take a generic one and, you know,

364
00:32:23,223 --> 00:32:24,944
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be covered, if you do it long enough.

365
00:32:27,285 --> 00:32:28,065
[SPEAKER_01]: It will address it.

366
00:32:30,766 --> 00:32:42,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that sutta, all the tense, means basically, if you go through all of those ways of penetrating, there is absolutely nothing for anyone in the world with the right view, that will be left.

367
00:32:44,892 --> 00:32:49,434
